43 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15 0 R
/E (           Many unwanted habits do not happen because people lack awareness; they happen because awareness often comes too late. After a regretful moment, a person may sincerely promise to change, but when temptation returns, that emotional force often weakens. This pattern relates to the hot-cold empathy gap and future self-continuity, where short-term impulses can override earlier intentions when the future self feels psychologically distant [1,2].)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
44 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15 0 R
/E (            Existing behavior-change systems often support planning, reflection, or restriction, but they still depend on willpower at the exact moment of temptation. Rewind is motivated by a simple question: could a system bring back how someone felt in the regretful moment, and use that replay to create a brief pause for reflection and reconsideration [3,4]?)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
45 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15 0 R
/E (Figure 1. Example use scenario of Rewind. After a late-night scrolling episode, the user records a regretful reflection. When the same temptation returns the next night, the recorded past self is replayed through the cat avatar to create a pause for reconsideration, leading to a better-rested next morning.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
